Inability to afford necessities, Financial psychological stress, Delayed medical presentation

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: SEV-002
Reason for Visit: Acute hypertensive crisis secondary to medication non-adherence due to financial constraints.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a patient with a severe headache, visual blurring, and a home blood pressure reading of 190/110. Assessment could not be safely delayed until regular clinic hours due to the immediate risk of acute target organ damage, including stroke or myocardial infarction.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My head is pounding, my vision is blurry, and I ran out of my blood pressure pills two weeks ago because I couldn't afford them."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a persistent inability to afford basic necessities, prioritizing food over prescription medications. Two weeks ago, the patient exhausted their supply of Ramipril and Amlodipine. Over the past 48 hours, the patient developed a worsening occipital headache and intermittent visual blurring. The presentation was delayed due to prohibitive out-of-pocket costs associated with medical transport and consultation fees. Chronic psychological stress related to financial insecurity has compounded the physiological strain.
Functional Status: Independent but currently restricted by severe headache.
Social/Safety Concerns: Substandard living conditions, inability to afford heating, and reliance on food banks leading to a high-sodium, low-nutrient diet.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 185/105 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 98% | Wt: 92 Kg
General: Appears older than stated age, anxious, clutching head, diaphoretic.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm, no murmurs. Neurological: Cranial nerves grossly intact, no focal neurological deficits. Fundoscopy reveals mild arteriovenous nicking but no papilledema. The clinical picture aligns with an inability to afford necessities, financial psychological stress, and delayed medical presentation.
Environmental Observations: Residence is notably cold with visible dampness; pantry contains predominantly high-sodium canned goods.
Medication Reconciliation: Confirmed complete cessation of Ramipril 10mg and Amlodipine 10mg for 14 days.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Poverty with acute hypertensive urgency - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Ischemic Stroke - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of unilateral weakness, facial droop, or speech slurring.

Subarachnoid Hemorrhage - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Gradual onset of headache over 48 hours, lacking the sudden "thunderclap" characteristic.

Migraine - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Lack of prior migraine history, bilateral occipital location, and direct correlation with severe hypertension and medication cessation.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with symptomatic hypertensive urgency directly resulting from medication rationing caused by poverty. Telephone triage identified symptoms of severe headache and visual blurring alongside profoundly elevated blood pressure, signaling impending end-organ damage. Immediate after-hours medical intervention was mandatory to initiate oral antihypertensive therapy and assess for acute neurological or cardiovascular compromise. Delaying this assessment until regular in-hours operations posed a critical, life-threatening risk of cerebrovascular accident.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Amlodipine 5mg stat. Prescribed one month of Ramipril 10mg and Amlodipine 10mg. Ordered comprehensive metabolic panel to assess renal function.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Step 1: Register for the government subsidized medication program utilizing the provided social work forms. This will reduce medication costs, immediately relieving financial anxiety and ensuring continuous access.
Step 2: Substitute one canned meal daily with a frozen vegetable mix. This actionable substitution will reduce sodium intake, rewarding the patient with improved blood pressure control and fewer headaches.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent referral to community social worker for financial counseling and utility assistance.
DME/Safety (Optional): Provision of an automated home blood pressure monitor via community grant.
Follow-up: Routine GP review within 72 hours for blood pressure reassessment.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department or calling 000 is required if experiencing chest pain, shortness of breath, sudden weakness on one side of the body, difficulty speaking, or loss of consciousness.

